description.p:
For -4 \lt x \lt 2, the pieces of f(x) are either linear or quadratic, and pass through the following points with the following behaviors: 





      on -4 \lt x \lt -3 a concave up parabola with vertex (-4,1) and ending at point (-3,3);






      on -3 \le x \lt -2, a linear function through (-3,3) and (-2,2), with a small open circle at (-2,2);






      on -2 \le x \lt 1 (except for at the value x=-1) a concave up parabola with vertex (0,-4) and passing through points  (-2,1) and (1,-3.25);






      at x = -1, there's a small open circle on the graph of f(x) at the point (-1,-3.25), and a small filled circle representing the point (-1,1) so that f(1) = 1;






      on 1 \le x \lt 2, a linear function through (1,-3.25) and (2,-2.5);






      finally, on 2 \lt x \lt 4, the function oscillates infinitely often between y = -1.5 and y = -3.5 as we approach a = 2 from the right.  The graph looks a bit like we took a large portion of the right half of the standard sine curve, say from x=0 to x=200\pi, and compressed the right end of the graph toward x=0, as one might do with a Slinky, and then shifted the resulting graph 2 unit to the right and 2.5 units down.  In addition, f(3) is not defined with a small open circle on the graph at the point (3,-2.5).
